WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.206 --> 00:00:02.302
Alright everybody, we did it.

00:00:02.572 --> 00:00:03.820
We swatted...

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:04.589
and shocked...

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:08.260
and smushed the invasive spotted 
lanternfly all summer long

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.900
and now that it’s fall we can surely say...

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:12.864
we’re done here.

00:00:12.864 --> 00:00:13.959
Mission accomplished.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.181
Right?

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:18.355
No! I'm so sorry!

00:00:18.355 --> 00:00:21.544
Unfortunately, it's not under control
at all.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.275
So, what the hell are we all doing?

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:36.940
This is Kristie.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.168
I'm the director of The Bug Chicks.

00:00:39.168 --> 00:00:40.231
And Jessica.

00:00:40.231 --> 00:00:42.745
I am the assistant director of
The Bug Chicks.

00:00:42.745 --> 00:00:44.487
They’re both entomologists.

00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:46.292
But let’s back up a second

00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:47.657
because if you’re not in the US

00:00:47.657 --> 00:00:51.833
this whole video so far is kind of...
unhinged.

00:00:53.103 --> 00:00:56.853
Spotted lanternflies are a type of insect
that’s native to parts of Asia.

00:00:56.853 --> 00:00:58.748
They're actually really beautiful.

00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:02.238
The adults have spotted wings 
with a bright red underwing.

00:01:02.238 --> 00:01:04.269
They’re super easy to recognize

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:06.609
and on some level: harmless.

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:07.924
They don’t sting or bite

00:01:07.924 --> 00:01:10.430
and in their native habitat
their population is controlled

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.040
by various predators.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.329
But when they're transported far away
from where they have evolved

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:18.450
then that's where we run into problems.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.904
We don't have natural enemies to
keep it under control.

00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:27.670
So their populations can just explode,
which is what we're seeing right now.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:31.130
Egg masses on a shipment of stone

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:34.850
are likely what brought the insect
to the US in 2012.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.481
But they were first found in
Eastern Pennsylvania in 2014.

00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:41.280
At that time, researchers tried to 
contain the population

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.035
and potentially wipe it out entirely.

00:01:44.035 --> 00:01:45.490
The state quarantined the area

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.990
requiring all businesses to have a spotted
lanternfly compliance permit

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.476
to ensure they weren't carrying
the bugs around.

00:01:51.476 --> 00:01:54.000
And told people to kill them on sight.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.380
Many researchers were initially
concerned these bugs

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.979
would destroy native forests because
they feed on tree sap.

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:04.860
They kind of jam these little pokey mouth
parts called rostrums into a plant

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.705
which is filled with sap and nutrients 
and they slurp it up.

00:02:08.705 --> 00:02:09.594
Also...

00:02:09.594 --> 00:02:12.945
Their poo is basically sugar water.

00:02:12.945 --> 00:02:15.760
That can lead to something called
sooty mold

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.758
which isn't harmful in and of itself
to people.

00:02:18.758 --> 00:02:21.305
But it sort of tamps down
on photosynthesis.

00:02:21.305 --> 00:02:23.907
So the plants have to work harder
to survive.

00:02:23.907 --> 00:02:27.000
Fortunately, the worst case scenario
didn’t come true.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.212
Lanternflies haven’t wiped out
full forests.

00:02:30.212 --> 00:02:33.480
Instead, the biggest losses are
to agriculture.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.081
Specifically orchards and vineyards.

00:02:36.081 --> 00:02:38.376
Vineyards in Pennsylvania have been 
hardest hit so far

00:02:38.376 --> 00:02:41.821
with some reporting up to a 90% loss.

00:02:42.472 --> 00:02:44.851
And now... they’ve spread.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.370
As of late 2022, spotted lanternflies have
been reported in 14 states.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.264
Thus the widespread summer-murder-campaign
on social media.

00:02:56.264 --> 00:02:59.939
At this point, it's too late to stop them
completely.

00:02:59.939 --> 00:03:02.640
Eradication is not on the docket anymore.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.353
We missed that boat.

00:03:04.353 --> 00:03:06.120
That ship sailed.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.567
And so now it's about mitigation
and management and control.

00:03:12.377 --> 00:03:15.159
Over time, some expect them to reach most
of the country

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:17.110
eventually spreading to places like California

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.269
where they could threaten a multi-billion
dollar fruit industry.

00:03:20.507 --> 00:03:24.000
Though, it could take them a few years
to get there.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.750
Thankfully, lanternflies don’t live
very long

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.250
and can only move about 4 miles
in their entire lives.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:31.440
Unless...

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.851
they hitch a ride.

00:03:32.851 --> 00:03:36.650
Which means the biggest culprit responsible
for this rapid spread

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:37.483
is us...

00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:39.255
in more ways than one. 

00:03:41.191 --> 00:03:43.760
Even before spotted lanternflies
came to the US

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.120
damage humans have done to
native ecosystems

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.320
set the stage for them to thrive.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.333
One example of this is their favorite tree 
to feed on: the tree of heaven.

00:03:52.761 --> 00:03:53.539
And yes.

00:03:53.539 --> 00:03:55.981
It’s also highly invasive.

00:03:55.981 --> 00:03:59.127
I mean, you want to talk about survivors.

00:03:59.127 --> 00:04:03.215
This plant, you cut it down and it's like,
"oh, yeah?"

00:04:03.215 --> 00:04:06.995
This tree was brought to the US
from China over 200 years ago.

00:04:06.995 --> 00:04:10.881
It thrives in urban environments
and areas with little vegetation.

00:04:10.881 --> 00:04:13.829
Like along railroads or highways
where it can act as a hotel

00:04:13.829 --> 00:04:17.102
for lanternflies as they make their way 
across the US.

00:04:18.483 --> 00:04:20.105
So, what now?

00:04:21.787 --> 00:04:23.590
Recently, predators like praying mantis

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.836
have started stepping in to help
level out the numbers.

00:04:26.836 --> 00:04:29.460
Other bugs like garden spiders, 
hornets, and wheel bugs

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.340
have also been seen attacking
spotted lanternflies.

00:04:32.864 --> 00:04:35.710
With the help of birds and
other insects, over time

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.631
populations will likely taper off
to more manageable levels.

00:04:39.631 --> 00:04:43.330
For now, the best method to manage 
spotted lanternfly populations

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.521
is simply to keep crushing them.

00:04:45.521 --> 00:04:49.575
Don’t resort to harsher methods
like home-made pesticides or fire.

00:04:49.575 --> 00:04:53.520
And when traveling, make sure to check your car 
for stray bugs or egg masses.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.270
Any of the ones that you didn't smack down
in the summer

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.940
have made it and they are right now,
like literally right now

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:06.400
they're laying eggs on any flat surface that
they can and they will overwinter

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.935
and then hatch out in the spring and
the whole system starts all over again.

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:15.009
If you see an egg mass, scraping it into a
bag with alcohol effectively kills it.

00:05:15.009 --> 00:05:17.259
But yeah, the scraping, the stomping.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:18.628
No flamethrowers.

00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:24.490
When we zoom out from just spotted lanternfly

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.675
we live in a world with potentially
4 million species of insect.

00:05:29.675 --> 00:05:33.289
And they are the decomposers,
they are the pollinators.

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:36.000
They are the recyclers of our world.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.258
And they create healthy soils
that grow the trees.

00:05:39.258 --> 00:05:43.819
They are integral to every system
on our planet.

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:46.160
The real impact of spotted lanternflies on
this system

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.641
might not be measurable yet.

00:05:47.641 --> 00:05:49.759
Ecosystems are wildly complicated

00:05:49.759 --> 00:05:53.612
and introducing a new species can have impacts
we’d never expect...

00:05:53.612 --> 00:05:57.672
on other bugs, on birds that eat bugs,
on future flora development.

00:05:57.672 --> 00:05:59.580
In the meantime, slowing down their spread

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.690
could help researchers devise new ways
to eliminate the bug safely.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.650
And give our native ecosystem what it needs:

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.157
time to catch up.

00:06:10.049 --> 00:06:11.553
They don't sting or bite.

00:06:11.553 --> 00:06:15.416
Yea, Kim, they don't sting or bite 
so there's nothing to be afraid of.

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:16.330
Come on--AH!

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.010
Now I'm going to feel bad 
about killing you

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.490
because I have an emotional
attachment to you.

00:06:24.812 --> 00:06:27.342
And you're gonna direct, 
you tell me, "now!"

